From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 11:40:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Taint the attribute text that attr() parses against type()
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

attr(name type(<syntax>)) parsed the attribute against the syntax with
an empty taint, so an attribute holding url("...") produced a <url>.
Every attr() substitution is attr()-tainted, and a tainted <url> is
invalid at computed-value time: the attribute must not choose what the
page fetches. An unregistered custom property kept the url, since only
a later property parse re-checked the taint.

The attribute text is now tainted as a whole while it parses, so a
<url> in it fails and attr() takes its fallback, or makes the
declaration invalid at computed-value time, as WPT's
css-values/attr-all-types expects.

https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/#attr-security

beep-boop-🤖
---
 custom_properties.rs | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/custom_properties.rs b/custom_properties.rs
index 04337b4..847c4f0 100644
--- a/custom_properties.rs
+++ b/custom_properties.rs
@@ -1921,13 +1921,16 @@ fn substitute_one_reference<'a>(
                                 ))
                             },
                             AttributeType::Type(syntax) => {
+                                // The attribute's text is attr()-tainted as a whole, so a <url>
+                                // in it fails to parse.
+                                // https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/#attr-security
                                 let value = SpecifiedRegisteredValue::parse(
                                     &mut parser,
                                     syntax,
                                     url_data,
                                     None,
                                     AllowComputationallyDependent::Yes,
-                                    AttrTaint::default(),
+                                    AttrTaint::new_fully_tainted(attr.len()),
                                 )
                                 .ok()?;
                                 let value = value.to_variable_value();
